  "preamble": "Labialization of the glottal fricative [h] to the labiodental fricative [f] adds a lip constriction, typically under the influence of a following rounded vowel. In the Shirongolic languages of Qinghai and Gansu, Proto-Mongolic initial *h- (attested as h- in Middle Mongol) became f- before rounded vowels in Mongghul (Monguor) and Santa (Dongxiang), while Central Mongolic lost it altogether.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Labialization, Fortition",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Proto-Mongolic to Mongghul (Monguor)",
      "languageFamily": "Shirongolic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "*hon 'year'",
          "to": "fon"
        },
        {
          "from": "*hunu- 'to ride'",
          "to": "funə-"
        },
        {
          "from": "*hünegen 'fox'",
          "to": "finiga, funige"
        },
        {
          "from": "*hüker 'ox'",
          "to": "fuguor (Monguor)",
          "note": "Before unrounded vowels h stays: *heligen > halige; Khalkha lost the h- (он, унах, үнэг, үхэр)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Proto-Mongolic to Santa (Dongxiang)",
      "languageFamily": "Shirongolic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "*hüker 'ox'",
          "to": "fugie"
        },
        {
          "from": "*hünegen 'fox'",
          "to": "funiegve, funiege",
          "note": "Before *u Santa keeps h- (*hulaxan > hulan) and Kangjia also has fünigo 'fox'."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
